title: Probe of Unauthenticated ARTEX Console Identification Endpoints
id: a23a1bdf-9110-4e34-9e98-017317726405
status: experimental
description: Detects requests for the two endpoints the ARTEX research uses to identify an exposed console, where GET /api/health confirms a panel is up and GET /api/auth/status reports whether setup is complete. Check Point counted more than 1,800 exposed consoles in early November 2026 and 1,608 live public instances, so enumeration of these paths is how both defenders and operators find the roughly 1,400 to 2,600 panels that hosted the LLM-driven credential-harvesting framework during the FBI FLASH alert window.
references:
  - https://blog.gitguardian.com/artex-fbi-alert/
  - https://research.checkpoint.com/2026/hacking-on-autopilot-inside-artex-cybercrime-campaigns/
  - https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/11/05/threat-actors-use-agentic-ai-tooling-to-find-and-abuse-exposed-secrets/
author: Tia N. List
date: 2026/11/19
tags:
  - attack.t1595
  - attack.t1190
  - attack.t1552
  - attack.t1528
logsource:
  category: webserver
detection:
  selection_health:
    cs-uri-stem|endswith: '/api/health'
  selection_status:
    cs-uri-stem|endswith: '/api/auth/status'
  condition: selection_health or selection_status
falsepositives:
  - Defensive scanning, internet-exposure inventories and CTI collection that deliberately enumerate ARTEX consoles to find them before operators do
  - Legitimate monitoring or load-balancer health checks on unrelated applications that happen to expose an /api/health path, so confirm the ARTEX console signature before acting
level: low
